n grab  it from Wolfram Alpha what it has though  is closing though so it only has each  one each day has the closing price what  I wanted to do is get a bit more  granular and get to the hourly level so  I went to a third-party website to grab  that so I've grabbed that here check our  dimensions and then we're gonna define  our dates price and I've also grabbed  volume because I'm kind of interested in  what's going on with volume as it  compares to sentiment on Twitter so we  have several options for kind of  breaking up chunks of text into  paragraphs sentence word letter its  stuttering Wolfram language since I'm  interested in the sentiment of each  tweet I'm gonna keep everything at the  tweet level but I'm gonna clean it up  just a little bit to remove stop words  and then also URLs so by default if  you've used the built in sentiment  classifier it's very simple  it returns values of positive negative  neutral what we're gonna do right here  is change those to 1 minus 1 and  determinant as 0 so we can better ease  with more easily visualize this so after  I've run my sentiment classifier we can  kind of take a quick look at it and see  what we have well open so we have a lot  of neutral that's likely because there  are still I didn't filter out as much as  I probably could have so we have a lot  of neutral going on but more positive  than negative for sure so now what we're  going to do is create a time series of  our sentiment values and time  and so what I've done here as well is  use mean filter to smooth some of this  out so if I did not apply this filter to  it and kind of do a moving average of  this we're gonna have a lot of noise and  not be able to see any kind of signal  going on in it so as you can see around  August if you'll recall Bitcoin price  went way up shot way up and kind of came  down so we can see our sentiment going  up right here you know what's  interesting and what will kind of get to  you is you know the question is what's  drivin